WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.118
Analysis starts with a useful question.

00:00:02.118 --> 00:00:04.137
A vague question produces vague analysis.

00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:06.492
If someone asks, what is going on,

00:00:06.492 --> 00:00:11.202
the analyst may collect a large amount of information without knowing which evidence matters.

00:00:11.202 --> 00:00:14.902
A stronger intelligence question identifies the decision to support, the audience,

00:00:14.902 --> 00:00:17.593
the timeframe, and the level of detail required.

00:00:17.593 --> 00:00:19.948
That structure turns curiosity into a requirement.

00:00:19.948 --> 00:00:22.976
An intelligence requirement should be tied to a decision.

00:00:22.976 --> 00:00:27.686
For example, a SOC manager may need to decide whether to tune a detection,

00:00:27.686 --> 00:00:31.723
escalate an incident, or assign more staff to a recurring alert type.

00:00:31.723 --> 00:00:36.432
A cloud engineer may need to decide whether a configuration pattern creates meaningful risk.

00:00:36.432 --> 00:00:40.469
An executive may need to decide whether a business process needs attention.

00:00:40.469 --> 00:00:44.843
A legal or compliance team may need a concise explanation of facts, assumptions,

00:00:44.843 --> 00:00:45.852
and response steps.

00:00:45.852 --> 00:00:48.879
The same information may support all of these audiences,

00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:50.898
but not in the same format.

00:00:50.898 --> 00:00:53.589
Audience matters because different stakeholders need different detail.

00:00:53.589 --> 00:00:56.953
Technical teams may need evidence tables, timestamps, indicators, data sources,

00:00:56.953 --> 00:00:57.963
and confidence notes.

00:00:57.963 --> 00:01:01.327
Leaders may need key judgments, implications, options, and risk tradeoffs.

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:05.364
Incident commanders may need what changed, what is known, what is uncertain,

00:01:05.364 --> 00:01:07.382
and what decision is needed now.

00:01:07.382 --> 00:01:10.746
A good analyst does not simply write everything they know.

00:01:10.746 --> 00:01:13.101
They shape the product to the decision.

00:01:13.101 --> 00:01:14.110
Timeframe also matters.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:18.484
A five-minute incident update is not the same as a two-week strategic assessment.

00:01:18.484 --> 00:01:22.184
Urgent analysis may need to state preliminary judgments and confidence clearly.

00:01:22.184 --> 00:01:25.212
Longer analysis may include more source evaluation, alternative hypotheses,

00:01:25.212 --> 00:01:26.221
and evidence review.

00:01:26.221 --> 00:01:30.931
Both can be professional if the analyst is clear about limitations and avoids overclaiming.

00:01:30.931 --> 00:01:32.949
Clarifying the question prevents wasted effort.

00:01:32.949 --> 00:01:36.313
Before collecting more data, ask what decision is being supported,

00:01:36.313 --> 00:01:39.677
who will use the answer, when the answer is needed,

00:01:39.677 --> 00:01:43.042
what level of confidence is required, what sources are authorized,

00:01:43.042 --> 00:01:45.396
and what would make the answer actionable.

00:01:45.396 --> 00:01:47.751
These questions do not slow analysis down.

00:01:47.751 --> 00:01:52.125
They prevent the team from spending hours on information that does not help.

00:01:52.125 --> 00:01:54.816
A strong requirement is not a bureaucratic formality.

00:01:54.816 --> 00:01:57.507
It is the anchor that keeps analysis relevant.

00:01:57.507 --> 00:02:01.208
It helps the analyst decide what to collect, what to ignore,

00:02:01.208 --> 00:02:05.917
how to organize evidence, how much detail to include, and how to communicate uncertainty.

00:02:05.917 --> 00:02:11.300
Good analysis begins when the question is clear enough that evidence can be judged against it.
